# Pinned uv resolution cutoff for this repo.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. uv stamps its resolution cutoff into uv.lock as
# `[options] exclude-newer`. A shell that exports a ROLLING cutoff (a supply-chain
# cooldown of "3 days ago", recomputed daily) therefore rewrites the committed
# lockfile every day, and the repo goes dirty for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the project. Pinning the value here fixes the recorded cutoff, so the lock
# stops churning.
#
# WHY .envrc AND NOT pyproject.toml. `[tool.uv] exclude-newer` exists and would be
# tidier, but it LOSES to the environment variable. Measured on uv 0.12.1,
# 2026-08-10, with three distinct dates and confirmed by the versions actually
# resolved, not merely by the string recorded:
#
#     --exclude-newer CLI flag   2023-06-01  ->  idna 3.4    WINS
#     UV_EXCLUDE_NEWER env       2026-08-07  ->  idna 3.18   beats pyproject
#     [tool.uv] exclude-newer    2020-01-01  ->  idna 2.8    only when env unset
#
# So a pyproject pin would look authoritative and be silently ineffective on any
# machine whose shell exports the variable: the worst outcome available. direnv
# loads this file on cd, after shell startup, so the project value wins.
#
# BUMPING IT. Change this date in the same commit as any dependency change, and
# run `uv lock` so the recorded cutoff matches. A pin never advances on its own,
# which is stricter than a rolling window and is the point; left stale for months
# it also means `uv add` resolves against a stale index. Bump deliberately.
#
# Nothing here is required to USE cc-warehouse, and CI needs neither this file nor
# direnv: the cooldown only bites when uv RESOLVES, and `uv sync` against a current
# lock does not resolve.
export UV_EXCLUDE_NEWER="2026-08-07T00:00:00Z"
